  "assumptions": [
    "Idealized model: small swings, period T = 2π√(L/g) with g = 9.81 m/s², exact for this model and only approximately true of a real pendulum.",
    "The string is light and doesn't stretch, the bob is treated as a point at the end of the string, and there's no air resistance or friction, so the swing never dies down.",
    "The swing starts at a fixed 10° angle. At larger angles a real pendulum's period gets a little longer, and that isn't shown.",
    "The swing angle is worked out from the timeline time, so changing the length or reaching the 60 s loop point can make the bob jump to a new place in its swing."
  ],
  "program": {
    "source": "const g=9.81,A=0.1745;\nexport function evaluate({inputs,time}){const L=inputs.length??1;const T=2*Math.PI*Math.sqrt(L/g);const th=A*Math.cos(2*Math.PI*time/T);return{nodes:{arm:{quaternion:[0,0,Math.sin(th/2),Math.cos(th/2)]},cord:{position:[0,-L/2,0],scale:[1,L,1]},bobN:{position:[0,-L,0]}},readouts:{period:T,len:L}};}"
  }
